e direct field accesses like `cnx_server->path[0]->first_tuple->p_local_cnxid->cnx_id.id` are also problematic since the Rust `Tuple` struct uses `Option<LocalConnectionIdToken>` instead of raw pointers, which means I'll need to work around the API limitations for these internal details.\n\nFor the more complex test functions like `multipath_trace_test_one` and `monopath_test_one`, I'll need to stub them out and check what imports from the C code are actually needed to make progress.\n\nLooking at `tls_api_one_scenario_init_ex`, I can see it's already available in util.rs and takes both client and server parameters along with simulated time, version, and initial connection ID, so that part should work.\n\nFor `monopath_test_one`, I need to create a `multipath_init_params` helper function that sets up default transport parameters with `initial_max_path_id = 2` and adjusts the timestamp setting. I'll add this to util.rs. I've also confirmed that the server connection fields like `nb_packet_holes_inserted` and `nb_crypto_key_rotations` exist, and the `loss_mask_default` field was already added to `TestTlsApiCtx` previously.\n\nNow I'm setting up the test flow: calling the connection setup function, initializing the multipath scenario, then running the data sending loop with the default loss mask applied.\n\nThe tricky part is that I need to clone the loss mask before passing it to the data sending loop since I'm already borrowing `test_ctx` mutably. So I'll extract it into a separate variable first.\n\nFor the overall structure, I should define the test ID enum or representation, stub out the helper functions across the modules, then translate each test function body one by one.\n\nActually, I can keep the test ID enum local to the multipath module rather than adding it to util. Now I'm sketching out the file structure with the necessary imports and module setup.\n\nSetting up the multipath.rs file... I think Approach A makes the most sense—keeping the multipath-specific logic contained in multipath.rs as private helpers, with `todo!()` stubs only for genuinely complex operations like `multipath_trace_test_one`. Now I'm working through the `multipath_test_one` implementation, starting with context initialization, link setup based on the test ID, and crypto configuration.\n\nWriting the test function... my analysis:\n- `multipath_init_params` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `wait_client_migration_done` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `wait_multipath_ready` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_test_do_keep_alive` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_init_callbacks` → in util.rs as todo stub (depends on MultipathTestId)\n- `multipath_verify_callbacks` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_init_datagram_ctx` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_set_datagram_ready` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_datagram_send_loop` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_verify_datagram_sent` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_test_abandon_cycle` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_verify_all_cid_available` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `multipath_trace_test_one` → in util.rs as todo stub\n- `Connection::get_peer_addr()` → in lib.rs as todo stub\n- `Connection::get_local_addr()` → in lib.rs as todo stub\n- `Connection::subscribe_to_quality_update_per_path` → in lib.rs as todo stub\n- `Connection::subscribe_new_path_allowed` → in lib.rs as todo stub\n- `aead_encrypt_mp` → in lib.rs as todo stub\n- `aead_decrypt_mp` → in lib.rs as todo stub\n- `Quic::set_default_spinbit` → in lib.rs as todo stub\n- `Quic::set_default_lossbit_policy` → may already exist\n- `Quic::use_predictable_random` field → check\n- `Quic::use_constant_challenges` field → check\n- `TestTlsApiCtx::path_events` field → doesn't exist\n- `TestTlsApiCtx::recv_ecn_server` field → doesn't exist\n\nNow I'm checking what else needs to be stubbed. The `multipath_trace_test_one` function is particularly complex in the C code—it sets up custom CID callbacks, spinbit and los