e new path. I'm realizing I can write full implementations in multipath.rs since it's in the tests/ directory—the constraint only applies to files outside tests/. So I'll keep the complex helpers that need substantial logic as stubs in util.rs, but implement simpler ones directly in multipath.rs. My plan is to add a set of stub functions to util.rs for the multipath-specific operations that would be tedious to translate from C or are used across multiple test files. Writing multipath.rs stubs...\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a structural mismatch in `multipath_test_one` — it's trying to access fields like `path[1]` and `first_local_cnxid_list` that don't map cleanly to the Rust implementation, and some fields like `path_events` don't exist in the test context yet, so I'll need to figure out what the actual field names and structures should be. For `multipath_trace_test_one`, there are numerous fields I need to stub out - callback contexts and functions on the server, seed arrays and random prediction flags on both client and server, complex nested path traversals to connection IDs, and a receive ECN field that doesn't exist in the Rust struct. This test is going to need multiple placeholder implementations to get it compiling.\n\nThe `multipath_init_callbacks` function is similarly involved - it opens files, enables path callbacks on both server and client connections, and subscribes to quality updates and new path notifications. Since `TestTlsApiCtx` doesn't have a `path_events` field in Rust, I'll stub this one out as well.\n\nFor `multipath_verify_callbacks`, it's just file comparison logic, so that's another stub. And `multipath_init_datagram_ctx` sets up datagram-specific context that doesn't map to the Rust struct either.\n\nMy approach is to add `todo!()` stubs throughout util.rs for all these multipath helpers, add the missing method stubs to lib.rs in the right impl blocks, then focus on writing the actual test bodies in multipath.rs.\n\nNow I'm thinking through where these new methods belong—`Connection::get_peer_addr` and `Connection::get_local_addr` go in the Connection impl, while the AEAD functions can live as standalone functions in lib.rs since I can't modify tls_api.rs directly.\n\nFor `multipath_test_one`, I need to understand how `PacketContext` maps from the C API—the test calls `queue_misc_frame` with a packet context parameter, so I'm checking what that type looks like in the Rust bindings.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1304,"cache_read_input_tokens":151717,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1304},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dd2036ff-0dd1-48ca-b8bf-92c20dcaef2a","uuid":"7defa01e-ebb5-4dc3-b935-d99e023d0d4e"}
